WEBVTT

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.759
Welcome back to the channel. Today we're

00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:13.200
breaking down one of the biggest

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.880
misconceptions in navigation. GPS does

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.119
not use triangulation.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:21.439
It uses trilateration.

00:00:21.439 --> 00:00:23.680
People mix these two all the time, but

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.640
they're completely different methods. By

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.880
the end of this video, you'll clearly

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.320
understand how GPS receivers calculate

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.440
your position, why satellites don't

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.800
measure angles, the exact meaning of

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.160
trilateration versus triangulation.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.239
Let's get started.

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:47.680
What GPS really measures. Your GPS

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.480
receiver, whether in your phone, car, or

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.039
drone, constantly listens to signals

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:55.760
broadcasted by satellites. Each

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.320
satellite sends two important pieces of

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.840
information.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.680
Its exact position in space, a timestamp

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.560
of when the signal was transmitted. When

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.119
your device receives that signal, it

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:10.960
checks how long the signal took to

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.920
arrive. Since radio signals travel at

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.640
the speed of light, we can convert that

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.000
time delay into distance equals time

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.360
speed of light. So the only thing GPS

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.280
actually measures is distance to

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.479
satellites, not direction or angle. This

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:33.360
is why GPS uses trilateration, the

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.000
method based on distances.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.600
Trilateration.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.720
Let's break trilateration down visually.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.200
Step one, one satellite, you are

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.079
somewhere on a sphere. If your receiver

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:49.360
knows it's exactly 20,000 km away from

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.479
satellite one, you could be anywhere on

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:54.560
the surface of an imaginary sphere

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.000
around that satellite. Step two, two

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.119
satellites intersection of two spheres.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:04.399
Now add satellite two. You now have two

00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:07.600
possible intersection curves like two

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.560
overlapping bubbles. Still not enough to

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.840
pinpoint you. Step three, three

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.599
satellites, two possible points. With

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:20.480
satellite three, the spheres intersect

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.200
at two points. Usually, one is

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.760
impossible. Maybe it's in space or on

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.760
the other side of Earth, so the receiver

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.959
eliminates it. Step four, four

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:34.959
satellites fixing time error. The fourth

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:37.920
satellite solves the biggest issue. Your

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.599
phone's clock is not perfectly

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:42.560
synchronized with satellite clocks. This

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.879
tiny timing error could lead to

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.680
kilometers of positional error. The

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.280
fourth satellite removes that

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.800
uncertainty and gives you a precise 3D

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.760
position. Latitude, longitude, altitude.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.160
That's trilateration in action. Pure

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.360
distance calculations.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.920
What is triangulation?

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.120
Triangulation is completely different.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.159
Triangulation uses angles, not

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:16.239
distances. It's commonly used in land

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:20.239
surveying, mapping with total stations,

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:23.280
radio direction finding, navigation

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.959
using known landmarks. To triangulate,

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:30.799
you measure angles between lines forming

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:32.400
triangles.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.760
GPS satellites however do not send angle

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.239
information and your phone cannot

00:03:38.239 --> 00:03:41.920
measure angles to satellites in the sky.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.319
That's why triangulation cannot be used

00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:48.159
for GPS. Why four satellites are

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:51.680
minimum? A common question why do we

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.519
need four satellites not three? Because

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:57.760
your devices internal clock is not

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.040
atomic. Even a tiny nancond difference

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.159
means huge errors. Three satellites

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:06.640
equals you get a location with massive

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.519
error. Four satellites equals correct

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:12.480
time plus correct location. This is why

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.680
a GPS fix normally requires at least

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.680
four satellites.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.120
So to summarize, GPS equals

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.800
trilateration,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.840
trilateration equals distances,

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.000
triangulation equals angles. GPS never

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.120
measures angles, only timing information

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.160
used to compute distance. If you learned

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.479
something new today, don't forget to

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:42.000
like, subscribe, and share this video

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.120
with other geospatial learners. See you

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.560
in the next tutorial.
